The solution to this issue has been proposed by employing nonthermal electron polarization in photoexcited triplet state at room temperature.10−15 In the typical scheme of DNP based on a photoexcited triplet (triplet-DNP, Figure 1a), spinselective intersystem crossing (ISC) produces a large electron spin polarization in the excited triplet state sublevels regardless of temperature,16 and this polarization is effectively transferred to nuclear spins by the integrated solid effect (ISE).17,18 Following its first report,10 room-temperature triplet-DNP has provided high enhancements in organic crystals such as pterphenyl with long spin−lattice relaxation time (T1).10−12,14
However, it remains difficult for such organic crystals to accommodate target molecules to be monitored. While amorphous solids such as $\textit{o}$ -terphenyl were employed as host matrices to accommodate target substrates, the flexible structure requires the cooling of the sample for triplet-DNP $(\sim120\:\mathrm{K})$ .13 Therefore, despite these efforts, it remains a grand challenge to develop a room-temperature triplet-DNP system with accessibility for polarizing targets. While pioneering works employed porous materials for low-temperature radical-based DNP to accommodate target molecules,19−24 there have been no examples of room-temperature DNP using porous matrices.
In this work, we show the first example of employing metal− organic frameworks $\left(\mathrm{MOFs}\right)^{25-33}$ as host materials for tripletDNP (Figure 1b). MOFs offer not only rigid crystalline structures but also nanopores for guest accommodation. For the proof-of-concept, it is necessary to satisfy the following conditions: (i) high dispersibility of polarizing agent in MOFs; (ii) long ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}~T_{1}$ of MOFs. Based on our previous work using a soluble pentacene derivative (6, 13-diphenylpentacene (DPP)) for triplet-DNP,34 we modified the typical polarizing agent pentacene with metal-coordinating carboxylate moieties $(\bar{4},4^{\prime}.$ - (pentacene-6,13-diyl)dibenzoic acid (PDBA)), Figure 2a) for its introduction into MOFs. A relatively long $^{1}\mathrm{H}~T_{1}$ has been reported for a prototypical diamagnetic $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ -based MOF, $\left[\bar{Z_{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}}}(\mathbf{MeIM})_{2}\right]_{n}$ (ZIF-8; $\mathbf{MeIM}=2$ -methylimidazolate).35−39 A partial deuteration of MeIM ligands allows the elongation of $T_{1}$ of partially deuterated D-ZIF-8. Polarization transfer from PDBA triplet electrons to $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ nuclei in D-ZIF-8 resulted in a clear enhancement ${\mathit{\check{\varepsilon}}}_{\varepsilon}={58}{\mathit{\check{\varepsilon}}}_{\varepsilon}$ ) of $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR signals of D-ZIF-8 at a low magnetic field of $0.67~\mathrm{T}$ and room temperature.
The dispersed accommodation of PDBA in ZIF-8 is further supported by fluorescence studies. Time-resolved fluorescence measurements of PDBA in DMF $\mathbf{\widetilde{\Gamma}}[\mathbf{PDBA}]=50\mu\mathbf{M})$ showed a single-exponential decay with a lifetime of 7.8 ns (Figure ${\mathrm{S4b}},$ Supporting Information). No detectable fluorescence was observed from bulk PDBA solids, probably due to the fluorescence quenching by singlet fission among aggregated PDBA molecules.40 On the other hand, ZIF- $^{\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8}\supset$ PDBA clearly showed fluorescence bands similar to those of the DMF solution (Figure $S4\mathrm{a}_{\mathrm{\lambda}}$ Supporting Information). The fluorescence decay of ZIF-8 ⊃ PDBA could be fitted with three components (2.0 ns $(6.3\%)$ , 6.1 ns $(56\%)$ , and 12.8 ns $(37\%)$ , Figure S4b, Supporting Information), which may reflect the partial aggregation of PDBA molecules.
The included amount of PDBA inside ZIF-8 was quantified from the UV−vis absorbance after digesting the composite with HCl in methanol. The inclusion amount can be controlled by changing the concentration of PDBA and $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The included amount of PDBA increased by increasing the NaOH concentration. As a control experiment, no uptake of nonionic DPP into ZIF-8 was observed, suggesting the accommodation of PDBA through coordination of carboxylate moieties to ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions of ZIF-8. With reference to previously optimized systems including 0.01−0.05 mol $\%$ of pentacene in dense $p$ -terphenyl crystals,12,13 we prepared ZIF-8 samples containing 0.036 $1\mathrm{{mol}\%}$ of PDBA.
The structure of ZIF-8 synthesized in the presence of NaOH ( $10\mathrm{\mol\}\%$ of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions) was confirmed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), elemental analysis, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption measurements. SEM images showed that the addition of NaOH decreased the crystal size from ca. $30\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S6, Supporting Information). This is in good agreement with previous reports about the effect of the base upon crystallization of ZIF-8.41,42 The smaller crystal size of ZIF-8 by base addition was explained by faster nucleation and growth of ZIF-8 due to the enhanced deprotonation of MeIM ligands. The presence of $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ did not affect the phase purity of ZIF-8, as confirmed by elemental analysis and PXRD patterns (Figure S7, Supporting Information). PXRD patterns showed peak broadening by the addition of ${\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}}$ , which agrees with the reduced crystal size. The $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for both ZIF-8 samples with/without NaOH showed $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ uptake in the lowpressure region $\left(P/P_{0}<0.1\right)$ , characteristic of microporous materials (Figure 2c). The BET surface area of ZIF-8 was mostly maintained when ${\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}}$ was used $(1280~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ compared with that of the pristine ZIF-8 $\left(1680\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\right)$ . Only ZIF-8 synthesized with $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ showed an additional ${\bf N}_{2}$ absorption in the middle-pressure region $\left(P/P_{0}>0.6\right)$ and a hysteresis in the desorption process. This behavior is typical for mesoporous materials, and the observed mesopores are ascribed to the grain boundaries between ZIF-8 nanoparticles. These PXRD and gas absorption results agree well with the previous reports of ZIF-8 nanocrystals.41,42
In practice, the spin−lattice relaxation time $\left(T_{1}\right)$ is the major factor in determining the attainable spin polarization since the polarization accumulation competes with the spin−lattice relaxation. To obtain the $^{\mathrm{~l~}}\mathrm{~H~}T_{1}\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~}~}$ value, we carried out solidstate magic angle spinning (MAS)-NMR measurements of
ZIF-8 at room temperature (Figure $\mathbf{\calS}8\mathbf{a}_{,\thickspace}$ , Supporting Information). Samples were packed in an Ar-filled glovebox to exclude the relaxation effect by paramagnetic oxygen molecules. The $^{1}\mathrm{H}~T_{1}$ value of ZIF-8 was estimated as $31\pm$ 0.1 s (Figure 2d).39 We confirmed that the inclusion of 0.036 mol $\%$ of PDBA did not significantly affect the $^{1}\mathrm{H}~T_{1}$ value of ZIF-8 $25\pm0.1\ s_{\mathrm{{\scriptscriptstyle1}}}$ , Figure $S8\mathbf{b}$ , Supporting Information). To further increase the $^{1}\mathrm{\check{H}}\ T_{1}$ value, we synthesized a partially deuterated MOF. For spin- $1/2$ systems, spin−lattice relaxation is mainly caused by fluctuating local magnetic fields at the site of nuclear spins due to the thermal motion of molecules.43 Generally, in solids, every spin is densely coupled to other spins and the $T_{1}$ values are averaged out over whole protons. Accordingly, we assumed that the rotation of methyl groups in ZIF-8 decreases the $^{1}\mathrm{H}\ T_{1}$ value and synthesized a partially deuterated ligand, MeIM- $d_{3}$ (Figure 2d, Scheme S2, SI), to construct methyl-deuterated ZIF-8 (denoted as D-ZIF-8). As expected, a longer $^{1}\mathrm{H}~T_{1}$ value of $53\pm1.0$ s was successfully obtained for D-ZIF-8 (Figures 2d, S8c, Supporting Information). In the following experiments, D-ZIF-8 ⊃ PDBA containing $0.036\mathrm{\mol\}\%$ of PDBA was used.
The generation of electron spin polarization in photoexcited triplet state was investigated by time-resolved electron spin resonance (ESR) measurements at room temperature. Under pulsed laser irradiation at ${589}\mathrm{nm}$ , the ESR spectrum of D-ZIF$^{8\supset}$ PDBA showed a characteristic line shape of spin-polarized triplet state at room temperature, in which the $\mathrm{T}_{1,0}$ triplet sublevel is populated preferentially and $\Delta m=1\mathrm{~T}_{1,0}\rightarrow\mathrm{T}_{1,-1}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{1,0}\rightarrow\mathrm{T}_{1,+1}$ transitions were observed as emission at 305 $\mathbf{m}\mathbf{T}$ and absorption at $355~\mathrm{mT}$ , respectively (Figure 3a). The signal intensity of the peak at $355~\mathrm{mT}$ showed microsecondscale decay profiles characteristic of the triplet excited state (Figure 3b). The negative components derived from $\mathrm{T}_{1,-1}\rightarrow$ $S_{0}/\mathrm{\bar{T}}_{1,+1}\to S_{0}$ transitions were also observed at around $50~\mu\mathrm{s},$ supporting the spin-selective ISC.44,45
The transfer of spin polarization from photoexcited triplet electrons to $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclei was evaluated by comparing the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR signal with and without the triplet-DNP process at 0.67 $\mathrm{\DeltaT}$ and room temperature (see Supporting Information for detailed measurement setup). PDBA molecules were photoexcited by a pulsed $589\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser $(500~\mathrm{Hz})$ , followed by microwave irradiation $\left(18.1~\mathrm{GHz}\right)$ and field sweep $\left(\pm100\ \mathrm{G}\right)$ for polarization transfer (ISE sequence, Figure 4a). The duration of microwave irradiation $(\bar{10}\mu s)$ was optimized based on the enhancement of the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR signal intensity. The extra time of $2~\mathrm{ms}$ was employed to avoid the sample degradation.
After repeating this process to accumulate the spin polarization, $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra were immediately measured. Because the accumulation of spin polarization by ISE competes with spin−lattice relaxation, the $\bar{}\mathrm{^{1}H}$ spin polarization becomes saturated by elongating the triplet-DNP process (Figure 4c). The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR signal intensity of D-ZIF- $s\supset$ PDBA showed a large enhancement factor of 58, and the signal-to-noise ratio of $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra was significantly improved by the tripletDNP process (Figure 4b). Besides, we investigated the tripletDNP process at a lower temperature $\left(220\mathrm{K}\right)$ , which is relevant to adsorption of the $^{129}\mathrm{Xe}$ gas probe.46,47 As a result, 85 and 102 times enhancement were achieved compared to the thermal equilibrium at $220~\mathrm{~K~}$ and room temperature, respectively. The change in the noise level of the NMR signal by reducing the temperature is mainly attributed to the thermal controller. The polarization buildup curve was fit to $A[1\mathrm{~-~}$ $\exp[-t/T_{\mathrm{B}})].$ , giving a buildup constant $T_{\mathrm{B}}$ of $13\mathrm{~s~}$ at $220~\mathrm{K},$ which was increased relative to room temperature (8.5 s). This indicates that the increase in the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR signal enhancement at low temperature is mainly due to the reduced molecular mobility and prolonged $\mathrm{^{1}H}\ T_{1}\$ . We note that D-ZIF- $^{\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8}\supset$ PDBA was unstable in repeated long-term laser irradiation, which would be solved by improving the dispersibility and chemical stability of the polarizing agent.
In conclusion, we demonstrate the first spin hyperpolarization of MOF protons based on triplet-DNP. The selective deuteration of the framework effectively suppressed the spin relaxation, giving a long $^{1}\mathrm{H}\ T_{1}$ value over $50\ \mathbf{s}.$ The newly synthesized polarizing agent PDBA allowed the generation of electron spin polarization by photoexcitation in D-ZIF-8. As a result, by applying triplet-DNP, the significant $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR signal enhancement factor $(\varepsilon)$ of 58 was achieved at room temperature. A further increase of the polarization enhancement factor would be achieved by improving the dispersibility and chemical stability of the polarizing agent and by elongating the MOF $T_{1}$ . This work proves the concept of using MOFs as “porous” and “rigid” hosts for triplet-DNP, and the polarization transfer to various guest target molecules is the obvious next step. This work provides an important initial step toward hyperpolarizing various molecules of interest in chemistry, physics, and biology at room temperature.
